{"text": " And a bond is not money. You can't hand a treasury bond to a currency dealer and ask for change. Currencies trade in pairs. If you want one to go up, you buy it and sell the other one. And because the US dollar sits on one side of nearly 90% of all currency trades on Earth, buying yen almost always means selling dollars. Of Japan's roughly 1.3 trillion dollars in reserves, about 930 billion sit in securities. Most of that is in American government bonds. Japan needed dollars and Japan sold American paper to get them. In May, Japan's holdings of American government debt fell by 66.7 billion dollars, the single largest one-month drop in Treasury holdings by any country in recorded history. America's long-term borrowing costs are at their highest level since 2007, and America's single largest foreign lender had just demonstrated in public that when its currency gets in trouble, it's willing to become a seller, which is a thing you'd rather your biggest lender not do, which becomes a supply problem in a market that is at a 19-year high.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 5.26, "text": " And a bond is not money. You can't hand a treasury bond to a currency dealer and ask for change.", "tokens": [50363, 843, 257, 6314, 318, 407, 1637, 13, 921, 460, 470, 1021, 257, 38115, 6314, 284, 257, 7395, 16456, 290, 1265, 329, 1487, 13, 50638], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13070792825812969, "compression_ratio": 1.5738831615120275, "no_speech_prob": 0.005940317176282406, "words": [{"word": " And", "start": 0.0, "end": 0.2, "probability": 0.25102341175079346}, {"word": " a", "start": 0.2, "end": 0.28, "probability": 0.9561256766319275}, {"word": " bond", "start": 0.28, "end": 0.62, "probability": 0.9929694533348083}, {"word": " is", "start": 0.62, "end": 0.86, "probability": 0.9915381669998169}, {"word": " not", "start": 0.86, "end": 1.1, "probability": 0.9982635378837585}, {"word": " money.", "start": 1.1, "end": 1.44, "probability": 0.998193